## 3. Results and discussion
Fig. 1 shows a schematic illustration of the synthesis process of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS. In brief, Co-ZIF-67 was first obtained by a rapid co-precipitation reaction of $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ and 2-methylimidazole in the methanol solvent. Subsequently, a distinctive morphology conversion of the parent ZIF-67 from a rhombic dodecahedron to an ultrathin cobalt-based MOF nanosheet was achieved by a solvothermal reaction in the presence of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ (Figs. S1a-d, SI). With the structural evolution, the characteristic peaks of the XRD profiles changed from the parent ZIF-67 to the newly developed CoMOF-NS, suggesting the occurrence of phase evolution (Fig. S2, SI), as is the case for the reported CoM-NS [39]. Finally, the Co-MOF-NS was converted to $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS via calcination treatment in air. XRD profiles (Fig. S3, SI) show that the characteristic peaks of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS at $18.9^{\circ}$ , $31.3^{\circ}$ , $36.8^{\circ}$ , $38.5^{\circ}$ , $44.8^{\circ}$ , $55.7^{\circ}$ , $59.4^{\circ}$ , $65.2^{\circ}$ and $78.4^{\circ}$ correspond to the (111), (220), (311), (222), (400), (422), (511), (440) and (622) planes of cubic spinel $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , respectively (JCPDS-74-2120). It is observed that enhanced calcination temperatures produce samples with higher crystallinities. In addition, the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -BK sample obtained by direct calcination of ZIF-67 at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ shows similar XRD diffraction patterns. It’s worth noting that the peaks intensity is weakened from $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -BK to $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS $(350^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ since the nanosheets lack long-range atomic order in the third dimension[40]. To further verify the constituent elements and the valence states of the elements in the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -BK sample, we performed XPS characterizations. As shown in Fig. S4a, the survey spectrum of both only display elemental peaks of C, Co, and O, and the inevitable presence of the C peak may be derived from adventitious carbon species. However, the positions of the core level orbitals of the cobalt element for Co3O4-NS show $0.3\mathrm{eV}$ shifts, to lower binding energy compared with that of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -BK. The binding energy shifts for the ultrathin nanosheets originate from the enhanced electron densities concentrated around the cobalt atoms， which owing to the dramatic distortion of the crystal structure[40, 41]. Furthermore, for the high-resolution spectrum of Co 2p (Fig. S4b,c, SI), in addition to the apparent satellite peaks (denoted as “sat.” in the figure), the binding energies at approximately 794.9, 779.9, 796.8 and $781.6\mathrm{eV}$ originated from the $\mathrm{Co^{3+}2p_{1/2},C o^{3+}2p_{3/2},C o^{2+}2p_{1/2}}$ and $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ spectra, respectively [42, 43], confirming the successful fabrication of
Co3O4. Moreover, except for the absorption bands of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ at 3473 and $1626~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ [43]， the FT-IR spectrum of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS displays two distinct absorption bands at 662 and 565 $\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ that are attributed to the stretching vibrations of the Co-O bonds [44], indicating that pure $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was prepared without attachment of any organic capping agents (Fig. S5, SI).
SEM and TEM measurements were conducted to examine the structure and morphology of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS. As displayed in Figs. 2a-b, the resultant $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS products maintain the nanosheet morphology that is inherited perfectly from their MOF precursors. Additionally, large amounts of mesopores can be observed on the ultrathin nanosheets. Notably, the morphology of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS collapses when the temperature reaches $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figs. S6a-c, SI). Additionally, the HRTEM image indicates a welldefined crystalline structure, in which the spacings between the lattice fringes with an angle of $60^{\circ}$ are $0.285\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding the planes perpendicular to the {111} facets of cubic spinel $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (PDF#74-2120) (Fig. 2c) [13]. Afterwards, the elementary mapping images clearly elucidate the uniform distribution of the $\mathrm{Co}$ and O elements (Fig. 2d). To gain further insights regarding the thickness of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS sample, three positions were selected in a $10{\times}10~{\upmu}\mathrm{m}$ area from the AFM image (Fig. 2e). As shown in Fig. 2f, the heights at positions 1, 2, and 3 in Fig. 2e correspond to $1.65\mathrm{nm}$ , $1.76\mathrm{nm}$ , and $2.03~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, further confirming the ultrathin 2D structure. For the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -BK sample, an obvious bulk structure originated from ZIF-67 was observed (Fig. S6d, SI). BET analysis indicates that the specific surface area of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}\mathrm{NS}$ is significantly improved compared to that of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -BK. For the various $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS samples, the specific surface area drastically decreases as the calcination temperature increases, which is mainly due to the decrease in the pore volume (Table S1, SI).
The catalytic performances of the as-synthesized $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS were evaluated by visible light driven $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction reactions performed in an acetonitrile $/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ mixture under mild reaction conditions with TEOA and $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]\mathrm{Cl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as the electron donor and photosensitizer, respectively. Fig. 3a shows the influence of the calcination temperature on the catalytic performance of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS. Apparent variations in the production rate of $\mathrm{CO}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ were observed for different samples. Comparison of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS samples calcined at 300 and $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ shows that the latter exhibits better gas evolution activity because the sample annealed at $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ has higher crystallinity (Fig. S3, SI), favouring the charge transfer in the photoredox reaction. However, the gas generation rate gradually decreases when the calcination temperature is further increased to 400 and $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\$ which is closely related to the significant decrease of the specific surface area of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS caused by the higher calcination temperature (Table S1, SI). Hence, we conclude that the crystallinity and specific surface area of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS sample synergistically affect the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction performance [18]. Therefore, the optimum calcination temperature is $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under the preparation conditions utilized in this study. Subsequent experiments were carried out on the samples calcined at this temperature.
The influence of the amount of the added $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS for the photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reaction was also examined. As shown in Fig. 3b, even with a tiny amount $\mathrm{(0.1~mg)}$ of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS added, the reaction system achieves the CO generation rate of $2.30\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-}$
1 and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ generation rate of $1.36\upmu\mathrm{mol}{}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . When $0.5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-NS}$ is included, the CO yield reached the maximum value of $4.52\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ together with the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ generation rate of $1.93\ \mathrm{\textmumol{\cdot}h^{-1}}$ . A further increase in the amount of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS clearly decreased the amount of evolved CO. This phenomenon is mainly attributed to the fact that an insufficient amount of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS lacks sufficient catalytically active sites, while excessive $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS can induce the light-shielding effect. Therefore, subsequent experiments were performed using $0.5\mathrm{mg}$ of added $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS. Then, the photocatalytic properties were evaluated with five different wavelengths of the incident light. As presented in Fig. 3c, the trend in the variation of the products is fundamentally coincident with the optical absorption spectrum of the $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)_{3}}]\mathrm{Cl_{2}}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ photosensitizer, rather than that of the Co3O4-NS sample (Fig. S7, SI). These results reveal that the photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction is indeed driven by the light excitation of the dye photosensitizer, producing excited electrons to realize the redox reactions, wherein the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS acts as a mediator to facilitate the transport of electrons to reduce the absorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO [7]. Meanwhile, the maximum AQE value for CO was calculated at $420\mathrm{nm}(0.71\%)$ , which is higher than that of many other reported systems (Table S2, SI).
A series of control experiments were performed to further investigate the essential factors that determine the performance of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction catalyst. When the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction reaction was carried out with $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS as the catalyst, the generation rate and selectivity of CO were $4.52\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ and $70.1\%$ , respectively, which are much higher than that of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -BK $(2.48\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , $58.4\%$ (Fig. 3d and its inset image, columns 1 and 2), indicating the importance of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ structure for the reaction. Similarly, Co-MOF-NS, as a precursor of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS, has higher reduction performance than ZIF-67, which is a precursor of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -BK, but both are significantly lower than $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS (Fig. 3d and its inset image, columns 3 and 4). Furthermore, sharp decreases in the CO generation rate $(0.14\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ and CO selectivity $(36.3\%)$ were observed if the catalyst was not added to the system. These phenomena highlight the crucial role of the catalysts in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction system (Fig. 3d and its inset image, column 5). No CO/H2 products are detected in the absence of visible light irradiation or photosensitizer (Fig. 3d, column 6 and 7), suggesting that the reaction is initialized by photocatalysis. When using Ar to replace $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to conduct the reaction under identical conditions (Fig. 3d, column 8), only negligible amounts of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ are detected $(1.22\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\$ ), and no detectable CO is generated. This result suggests that the generated CO originates only from the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reactant. Additionally, a control experiment was conducted without the existence of TEOA, and no generation of gas products was detected (Fig. 3d, column 9), revealing that the sacrificial agent markedly affects the performance of the catalyst [33, 45-47].
As shown in Fig. 3e, a linear relationship is observed between the $\mathrm{CO}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ yield and the irradiation time over the first $6\textup{h}$ of the reaction. Thereafter, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-CO conversion rate progressively decreases, which can mainly be attributed to the exhaustion of the dye photosensitizer after a long-term reaction [7, 45, 47, 48]. The accumulated evolution of the product is ${\sim}45~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ after photoirradiation for $10\mathrm{~h~}$ . To verify this deduction, the same amount of fresh dye was added into the system after 6 h irradiation, and the reaction was continued under the same conditions. Fig. 3f shows that the CO generation retains ${\sim}90\%$ of its original value after five runs. Simultaneously, the XRD characterization of the tested samples (Fig. S8, SI) was also conducted to gain a better understanding of the stability. No obvious differences between the tested sample and fresh sample were observed, indicating the superior stability and reusability of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS in the photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction system. Moreover, as shown in Figure S9, after photocatalytic reaction in the $^{13}{\bf C O}_{2}$ $\mathrm{(m/z=45}$ ) atmosphere, mass spectrum signals of $^{13}\mathrm{CO}$ $\mathrm{Im}/\mathrm{z}=29,$ ) was clearly observed, indicating that the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was the source of the reduction products.
Photoelectrochemical characterizations were carried out to elucidate the origins for the higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction property of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}\mathrm{NS}$ than $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -BK from the perspective of charge transfer kinetics. Firstly, the time-resolved PL decay spectroscopy were employed to reveal the dynamic charge behaviour in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction systems. The emission of $\mathrm{[Ru(bpy)_{3}]C l_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ in solution follows one exponential decay with a lifetime of 367.12 ns (Fig.4a). However, the PL lifetime became shorter when the catalysts were added, and the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS (309.18 ns) is shorter than that of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -BK (330.16 ns). Also, the PL measurements were investigated to study the separation and recombination rates of the photoexcited charges. As presented in Fig. S10, the PL intensity is greatly diminished in the presence of the catalysts compared to the blank system. Furthermore, the emission intensity of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS is lower than that of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}.$ -BK. The obvious lifetime decrease and PL quenching principally indicates the remarkably suppressed electron-hole recombination [14, 49, 50] that augments the utilization of photoinduced electrons, thereby improving the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-CO conversion. Conversely, the EIS spectra show that $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS exhibits an obviously smaller semicircle in the Nyquist plot (Fig. 4b), indicating a lower charge-transfer resistance in the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS system that permits fast transport and separation of interfacial charge. These results suggest that $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS have a better charge transport capability which is beneficial for increasing the photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction efficiency.
To further explore the factors that lead to the various performance characteristics of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction, ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption experiments were conducted for $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -BK. As presented in Fig. 5c, over the entire relative pressure range $\left(\mathrm{P/P_{0}}\right)$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS $(279.76\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ shows a much higher ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption capacity than the Co3O4-BK $(91.85~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , mainly due to large amounts of mesopores with the pore sizes of approximately $3\mathrm{nm}$ distributed on the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS sample (inset in Fig. 4c) and the specific surface area value of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}–N S(76.11c m^{3}g^{-1})}$ sample is approximately 4 times higher than that of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -BK $(19.89~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1},\$ ). The structural features of the high specific surface area and well-developed porosity allow $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS to expose many more active sites for the adsorption and activation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. Therefore, to further support this hypothesis, a $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption experiment was performed (298 K, 1 atm). As observed from Fig. 4d, the adsorption performance of Co3O4-NS sample (5.41 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is approximately 3 times higher than that detected for $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -BK (1.93 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ), which is conducive to the photochemical conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
To obtain an in-depth understanding of the influence of the structural features on the photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, we performed DFT calculations to evaluate the adsorption energy (Ea) of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules on two $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ crystal models: monolayer and bulk. Fig. 5 shows the optimized configurations of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules adsorbed on the surface of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ monolayer and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ bulk. Among these, the most energetically stable configurations of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ absorbed on the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ bulk is that with one O atom coordinated with one surface Co atom (Fig. 5a), and the O-Co bond length of 2.28 Å. However, the electrons in the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ monolayer are more active and have a stronger ability to adsorb gas molecules [51], thus, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule on the surface of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ monolayer is optimized to form O-Co bond and C-O bond, with the lengths of 1.94 Å and 1.46 $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , respectively (Fig. 5b). In particular, the calculated Ea values of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ bulk and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ monolayer were -0.13 and -0.27 eV, respectively. It is well-known that a more negative Ea value indicates a stronger CO2 adsorption ability on the catalyst surface [52, 53]. Meanwhile, we also performed DFT calculations to evaluate the Ea of H atom on two $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ crystal models (Fig.S11). The calculated values of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ bulk and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ monolayer were -0.16 and $-0.10\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively, which indicate a weaker H atom adsorption ability on $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ monolayer (the relevant reasons were explained in the supporting information). These results suggest that $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ monolayer is indeed more likely to adsorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules under the same conditions. It should be stressed that we currently cannot rule out possible effect of crystal facets. Nevertheless, based on the fact that the more active electrons in the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ monolayer, we conclude that the ultrathin nanosheet structure of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ could bring a higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption energy during the photoreduction $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reaction, which is more conducive to the photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction.
## 4. Conclusions
We demonstrated a flexible, efficient and controllable protocol of synthesizing CoMOF-NS-derived ultrathin $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanosheets for high-efficiency photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. The prepared $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS retains the distinctive structural nature of 2D structure and well-developed porosity that 1) provide a large specific surface area and high affinity to promote $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, 2) shorten the diffusion length to facilitate the separation and transport of charge carriers, and 3) expose many more active sites for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. Consequently, during the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction process, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}.$ - to-CO conversion rate of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS $(4.52\ \upmu\mathrm{mol\cdoth^{-1}}$ ) was considerably higher than that of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -BK $(2.48\ \upmu\mathrm{mol\cdoth^{-1}},$ ), and the corresponding selectivity was increased from $58.4\%$ to $70.1\%$ . Meanwhile, the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -NS exhibits a high AQE value $(0.71\%)$ and excellent stability in the reaction system. Moreover, DFT calculations reveal that the model $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ monolayer has a stronger $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption energy $\mathrm{(-0.27~eV)}$ than the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ bulk $(-0.13\ \mathrm{eV})$ , which is beneficial for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-CO conversion. Hence, we trust that this MOF-engaged strategy will open up new opportunities to rationally construct a large number of advanced ultrathin holey nanosheets for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.
